+///   `test_signature_node_update`) entirely, so every entry in every subtree
+///   is visited and individually audited. This is still sound as an audit
+///   strategy: `Augmentation::sig_and` is the bitwise AND (a subset) of every
+///   stored entry's signature in the subtree and `sig_or` the bitwise OR (a
+///   superset), so `test_signature_node`/`test_signature_node_update`
+///   rejecting a node is implied by *every* entry in that subtree
+///   individually failing the corresponding per-entry `test_signature` /
+///   `test_signature_update`. Per-entry soundness therefore implies node
+///   soundness, so skipping the node-level check in audit mode only costs
+///   time (it revisits entries a sound node reject would have skipped) and
+///   never hides a filter bug that per-entry auditing would have caught.
+/// * whenever a per-entry signature or histogram filter would have rejected
+///   a candidate, does not skip it: it instead runs the exact arbiter
+///   (`Dir::test_precise_packed` / `Dir::test_precise_packed_update`) with
+///   `apply_hist_filter = false` anyway. If the exact test would have
+///   accepted a pair the filter rejected, that is a soundness bug in the
+///   filter, so the violation is counted, logged, and the process panics.
+/// * otherwise behaves exactly as without audit mode, so enabling it must
+///   never change any search result -- only its cost.
+pub fn audit_enabled() -> bool {
+    *AUDIT.get_or_init(|| env_flag("SORTNETOPT_FILTER_AUDIT"))
